Subject: register VM: make lazy bundle loading work

What this does:
  Fixes two bugs that made every JS_READ_OBJ_LAZY read of a register bundle
  fail (the path a React Native app ships):

  1. bc_intern_atom read the atom's record at s->buf_start + offset but bounded
     the read by the lazy source's copy of the buffer. During the eager read
     those are two different allocations (the caller's buffer and the copy), so
     the read failed with "read after the end of the buffer". The stack VM
     never hit this because it interns atoms only at materialization, when the
     reader runs over the copy; the register program's atoms are mapped during
     the eager read. Both pointers now come from the lazy source.

  2. regvm_validate_code bounded atom operands by rt->atom_count, the number of
     live atoms, but an atom index ranges over the whole table including free
     slots (rt->atom_size). Lazy interning reuses free slots and hands out
     indices above the live count, so valid programs were rejected with
     "register atom out of range".

Measured: pdfjs register bundle, 30 loads, x86-64 clang-19 -O2: eager 0.70 s,
  lazy 0.11 s (failed before). All 13 Octane rows run from a lazy register
  bundle with output identical to the eager bundle apart from scores; box2d
  checksum 546.1008391043414.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -73246,9 +73279,12 @@
     JSAtom atom;
     uint8_t type;
 
-    s->ptr = s->buf_start + s->atom_offset[idx];
-    if (s->lazy_src)
+    if (s->lazy_src) {
+        s->ptr = s->lazy_src->buf + s->atom_offset[idx];
         s->buf_end = s->lazy_src->buf + s->lazy_src->buf_len;
+    } else {
+        s->ptr = s->buf_start + s->atom_offset[idx];
+    }
     s->ptr_last = NULL;
 
     if (bc_get_u8(s, &type))
@@ -73631,7 +73667,9 @@
     const uint8_t *code = b->regvm_code;
     int len = b->regvm_len;
     int wide = 1;                       /* register operands are two bytes */
-    uint32_t atom_count = ctx->rt->atom_count;
+    /* atom indices range over the whole table, free slots included, so the
+       bound is its size, not the number of live atoms */
+    uint32_t atom_count = ctx->rt->atom_size;
     uint8_t *is_start = NULL;
     int *targets = NULL;
     int p, i, n_targets = 0, rc = -1;
